{"name":"Keto / ketogenic diet","deep":true,"image":{"url":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/64/Ketogenic_diet_over_12_months.png/960px-Ketogenic_diet_over_12_months.png?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ketogenic_diet_over_12_months.png","caption":"Ketogenic diet over 12 months","source":"Wikimedia Commons"},"mentions":11,"casual":{"lead":["The ketogenic diet is a high-fat, adequate-protein, very low-carbohydrate diet. It was developed as a medical therapy for refractory epilepsy, mainly in children, and is now popular for losing body fat and getting a leaner face and body. Some people also report it calming acne.","With little carbohydrate, the liver makes ketone bodies that replace glucose as fuel, and appetite often drops, so body fat falls. Wikipedia reports low-carbohydrate diets produce somewhat more short-term weight loss than low-fat diets, with the advantage fading over time, and a 2022 Cochrane review found no benefit over balanced diets at two years. Acne effects are not covered in the sources consulted."],"sections":[{"h":"How people use it","paras":["People cut most bread, pasta, sugar and starchy foods and build meals around meat, fish, eggs, dairy, oils and low-carb vegetables, often tracking carbs to stay in ketosis. Early weight changes are partly water and show within the first weeks."],"subs":[]},{"h":"Risks & side effects","paras":["Reported effects include constipation, early fatigue, raised lipids and, rarely, ketoacidosis, with case reports documented. People with diabetes, pregnancy or medical conditions are generally advised to consult a clinician first."],"subs":[]}]},"gallery":[{"url":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/64/Ketogenic_diet_over_12_months.png/960px-Ketogenic_diet_over_12_months.png?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","full":"https://upload.wikimedia.